<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bangor Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 558</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bangor Abbey was a monastery on the southern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> Lough in County Down, Northern Ireland, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-comgall">Saint Comgall</a> around 558. It became one of the great schools of early Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, sending out missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> and producing the Antiphonary of Bangor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids devastated it in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey.jpg" alt="Bangor Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Notafly · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BangorAbbeyAD688.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Comgall was born in Antrim in 517 and educated at Clooneenagh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a>. He withdrew for a time to a lonely island before his friends persuaded him to return. The annals give dates for the foundation between 552 and 559, and the earliest, the Annals of Tighernach and the Annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inisfallen">Innisfallen</a>, give 558. The name was sometimes written Beannchor, and the place was called the Vale of Angels after a legend that Saint Patrick had rested there and seen the valley filled with angels.</p><p>Comgall's rule was strict: prayer and fasting were constant, food was scant and plain, and worship, with a large part for music, came first in the life of the community. Within the rampart that enclosed the buildings, students studied scripture, theology, logic, geometry, arithmetic, music and the classics. Bangor is regarded as second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> among early Irish monastic sites, and Robin Flower thought the earliest Irish chronicle was probably written there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bangor trained many missionaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthach-of-lismore">Carthach of Lismore</a> and Fintan of Doon studied there, and Saint Mirin was prior before founding Paisley Abbey. Columbanus, thought to have been taught by the fifth abbot, Mo Sinu moccu Min, left for continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with Gall in 590, and the two founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxeuil-abbey">Luxeuil Abbey</a>, St Gallen and Bobbio. The Antiphonary of Bangor, a book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymns, prayers and antiphons probably written by the monks between 602 and 691, was kept at Bobbio for over 1,000 years.</p><p>The buildings, then probably of wood, were burned in 616 and 755, and between 822 and 824 the Norsemen plundered the monastery, striking down learned men and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and carrying off the shrine with Comgall's relics. Its importance declined late in the 10th century. Saint Malachy, abbot from 1121, had to rebuild everything, but three years later he became bishop of Down and Bangor decayed again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> held it in 1469 and Augustinians a century later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the dissolution of the monasteries James I gave the site to Sir James Hamilton, who repaired the church in 1617 and was buried in it in 1644. St Malachy's Wall is all that remains of the abbey ruins, and the church tower dates from the 14th century. The shaft of a high cross of about the 8th century, found in the abbey grounds, is built into a wall of the private chapel at Clandeboye.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bangor Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bangor</b>, traditionally founded by Saint Deiniol in the sixth century, became the seat of the diocese, while Clynnog Fawr, associated with Saint Beuno, Penmon and Holyhead were important churches.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their wealth lay in the fertile lands of Lecale and the Ards, and the monasteries of <b>Bangor</b> and Movilla lay nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-fiatach">Dal Fiatach</a></li><li><blockquote>St Comgall, founder of the monastery of <b>Bangor</b>, belonged to this people.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's monasteries, including <b>Bangor</b>, Movilla and Nendrum, were among the great centres of early Irish learning.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Kingdom of Ulaid</a></li></ul></section>
